Choose up to {{ most }} sums that would change the advice. Ask of each one: if it came out differently, would we tell them something different? If not, leave it out.

The sums that usually matter:
- what the money actually comes to at the level they are aiming for, and what they keep of it
- whether that covers what they need to earn, and what is left over for everything else
- how long the money they have will last
- how much they would have to sell to break even, or to reach what they need
- what one customer, one booking or one lost sale is worth

Each sum is one operation:
- "product": multiplies its inputs
- "total": adds inputs of the same unit
- "difference": the first input less the rest, same unit
- "ratio": exactly two inputs, the first divided by the second

Inputs are figure ids from the list above, or the id of an earlier sum. Your sums are numbered in order: the first is SUM-1, the second SUM-2. A percent can be an input to a product as it stands.

Never write a number into a sum. Only if a sum needs the calendar, give it in "constants": 7 days a week, 12 months a year, 30 days a month, 52 weeks a year, 365 days a year.

If the figures do not support a sum, leave it out. Never use a figure for something it does not measure, and never add unlike things.

"label" says in a few plain words what the result is, in their terms ("what you keep at 500 bookings a month"). "unit" is the unit of the result, such as "AED", "bookings" or "months". "period" is "day", "week", "month", "year" or null.

